<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=cotton-gin">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Tools and materials</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cotton gin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">5th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> gin is a machine that separates cotton fibres from their seeds; the earliest evidence of it is a single-roller gin depicted in 5th-century Buddhist paintings in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajanta-caves">Ajanta Caves</a> in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Two-roller gins appeared in India and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> between the 12th and 14th centuries, and the Indian worm-gear roller gin is still in use. In 1793 the American <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eli-whitney">Eli Whitney</a> created a mechanical gin for short-staple cotton, patented in 1794; it made cotton farming more profitable and increased the demand for enslaved labour in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-south">American South</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cotton-gin.jpg" alt="Cotton gin" width="320" height="210" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tom Murphy VII · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cotton_gin_EWM_2007.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>5th century</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Ajanta Caves, India</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The modern cotton gin is credited to Eli Whitney in 1793, though some credit Catharine Littlefield Greene with suggesting its brush.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">Cotton<span>c. 6000 BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=cotton-gin">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
